--TEST--
`Core\Xml::parse` throws on a document that is not well-formed, and a document type declaration is refused whole rather than resolved — the opposite contract to the never-failing WHATWG parser on the same node family, `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every way one document can fail to be well-formed, each refused with a
// sentence naming what was written. Nothing here answers a partial tree:
// `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused` is why a document that does not parse
// produces nothing rather than as much of one as could be built.
array<string> $malformed = [
    "an element closed as something else" => "<a></b>",
    "an element never closed" => "<a><b></b>",
    "a closing tag with nothing open" => "</a>",
    "no root element at all" => "<!--only a comment-->",
    "a second root element" => "<a/><b/>",
    "text outside the root" => "<a/>trailing",
    "an unquoted attribute value" => "<a k=v/>",
    "an attribute with no value" => "<a k/>",
    "a tag with no name" => "<1a/>",
    "a comment holding a double hyphen" => "<a><!-- -- --></a>",
    "a CDATA closer written in content" => "<a>]]></a>",
    "the empty document" => "",
];

foreach ($malformed as string $why => string $document) {
    try {
        Core\Xml::parse($document);
        echo $why, ": accepted\n";
    } catch (ParseError $refused) {
        echo $why, ": refused\n";
    }
}

// The one refusal worth printing whole, because it is the class's answer to
// XXE: there is no code path here that resolves an entity, so an external
// subset is never something that could have been fetched and an internal one
// defines nothing that could be expanded.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<!DOCTYPE a SYSTEM \"http://example.invalid/a.dtd\"><a/>");
    echo "a document type declaration was read\n";
} catch (ParseError $doctype) {
    echo $doctype->message, "\n";
}

// And an internal subset, which is the half that is legal XML and still gets
// nothing: a declaration that defines an entity is refused at the declaration,
// before there is anything to expand.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY x \"y\">]><a>&x;</a>");
    echo "an internal subset was read\n";
} catch (ParseError $internal) {
    echo $internal->message, "\n";
}

// What survives all of that is the ordinary document, which parses.
Core\Xml\Node $fine = Core\Xml::parse("<a><b/></a>");
echo "well formed: ", Core\Arr::count($fine->children()), " child\n";
--EXPECT--
an element closed as something else: refused
an element never closed: refused
a closing tag with nothing open: refused
no root element at all: refused
a second root element: refused
text outside the root: refused
an unquoted attribute value: refused
an attribute with no value: refused
a tag with no name: refused
a comment holding a double hyphen: refused
a CDATA closer written in content: refused
the empty document: refused
Core\Xml::parse(): a document type declaration is not read at all — no entity is resolved from an external subset, from an internal subset or from the network, so there is nothing a `<!DOCTYPE …>` could mean here.
Core\Xml::parse(): a document type declaration is not read at all — no entity is resolved from an external subset, from an internal subset or from the network, so there is nothing a `<!DOCTYPE …>` could mean here.
well formed: 1 child
